\section{Annotating chunk languages: the autolang filter}
\label{sec:autolang}

The noweb Hacker's Guide reserves an [[@language]] keyword in the
pipeline grammar and sketches how it should be filled in: tools
identify the language of root chunks from their names, and a filter
tags the remaining chunks by propagating from uses to definitions.
That filter was never written---until now.  [[autolang]] reads the
markup stream, infers a language for every code chunk it can, and
writes the stream back out with an [[@language]] line added to each
such chunk, for the filters downstream to consume.

The first consumers are noweb's autodefs filters, which use the
annotations to skip chunks written in languages they do not
understand, so that the identifier index of a mixed-language document
no longer collects, say, Makefile variables that happen to match
Python's assignment syntax.  [[autolang]] must therefore run
\emph{first}, before any filter that wants language information.
[[noweave]]'s [[-autolang]] option arranges that (a plain [[-filter]]
could not: noweave always places autodefs filters before the
[[-filter]] stages):
\begin{verbatim}
noweave -autolang -autodefs python3 -autodefs make -index fib.nw > fib.tex
\end{verbatim}
The backends predate the keyword and ignore [[@language]] lines, so
the annotations are harmless wherever nothing consumes them.

Unlike noweb's other filters, which are written in Icon or awk,
[[autolang]] is a Python~3 program.  It needs nothing beyond the
standard library: Pygments, when it happens to be importable, extends
the built-in filename tables (Section~\ref{sec:pygments}), but is not
required.

\subsection{Overview}

The program copies the markup stream from standard input to standard
output in two passes over the (buffered) stream:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Determine the language of every code chunk.  A chunk whose name
      looks like a filename (e.g.\ [[fib.py]] or [[Makefile]]) gets
      the corresponding language; every other chunk inherits the
      language of the chunks that use it.  We must infer languages
      ourselves because noweb's [[markup]] emits no language
      information---the chunk names are the only hint there is.
      Further seeds---a chunk's own shebang line, and the lexical context
      a chunk is \emph{used} in---are described in
      Section~\ref{sec:context}.
      An annotation already present in the stream is honored as
      authoritative.
\item Copy the stream, inserting an [[@language]] line into every
      code chunk whose language is known (Section~\ref{sec:annotate}).
\end{enumerate}

Languages are named by Pygments lexer aliases ([[python]], [[make]],
[[bash]], \dots), which agree with the Hacker's Guide's suggested
names for all the common languages except where the synonym table of
Section~\ref{sec:langs} translates.  A chunk used only inside Python
string literals is annotated [[text]]: it contains prose, not code,
and every consumer treats it accordingly (Section~\ref{sec:strings}).

The inference is kept in chunks of its own, [[inference constants]]
and [[inference functions]], apart from the annotation logic of
Section~\ref{sec:annotate}.  A filter that needs the same language
map---a highlighter choosing a lexer per chunk, say---can then tangle
from this file alongside its own source, since [[notangle]] accepts
several files, instead of duplicating the code.  The [[tominted]]
filter (\texttt{tominted.nw}) does exactly that.

\subsection{Program layout}


<<autolang>>=
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Noweave filter that annotates code chunks with their language.

Reads a noweb markup stream on stdin and writes it to stdout,
adding an ``@language`` line to every code chunk whose language
can be determined---from filename-like chunk names, shebang
lines, and the lexical context each chunk is used in (heredoc
bodies, interpreter ``-c`` arguments, make recipes), propagated
transitively through chunk uses.  Run it as the first filter,
before any filter that consumes language information (e.g. the
autodefs filters).  See nowebfilters(7).
"""

import sys

<<inference constants>>
<<inference functions>>
<<autolang functions>>

if __name__ == "__main__":
    autolang_main()
@

\subsection{Mapping chunk names to lexers}

A root chunk in a tangleable literate program is conventionally named
after the file it produces, so the chunk name is our language oracle.
We recognize both bare filenames ([[fib.py]]) and the
LaTeX-safe quoted convention (the chunk name itself wrapped in noweb's double square
brackets), which we unwrap before matching.

The lookup has three levels.  First come user-supplied regex
rules, matched against the whole chunk name; a rule is how a
labeled chunk---one whose name puts a word such as \emph{test}
before a quoted file reference---acquires a language, since such a
name is not itself a filename, and a matching rule also overrides
the tables (Section~\ref{sec:mappings}).  Next come two static tables,
mapping either an exact basename or an extension to a Pygments
lexer name; additional table mappings can be supplied as
command-line arguments too.  Last, when neither rules nor tables
match, we ask Pygments itself, which knows the filename patterns of
every language it can highlight (next section).

Why keep tables at all, when Pygments already knows the answer?
Because Pygments need not be installed where \emph{this filter}
runs: the filter runs in the noweave pipeline, while Pygments is
only guaranteed wherever the woven document is later compiled, and
those can be different machines.  The tables keep the popular
languages working with no dependencies beyond Python itself.  They
also pin \emph{which} lexer alias we use where Pygments offers a
choice (e.g.\ [[perl]] rather than [[prolog]] for [[.pl]]), and
they are what the command-line mappings extend, so a user override
always beats Pygments' opinion.

