User-supplied rules live in a separate list rather than the tables
because they are tried in order and matched as regexes, not looked
up by key.  The empty default leaves the filename inference
unchanged, which is what keeps a labeled chunk unlabeled until a
rule asks for otherwise.

<<inference constants>>=
LEXER_RULES = []   # list of (compiled regex, lexer alias)
@

A matching rule wins before any filename logic.  Failing that, the
quoting convention is unwrapped and the basename is looked up,
first verbatim, then by extension, and finally against Pygments'
own filename patterns.  Returning [[None]] means ``no idea'', which
pass~1 may still resolve by inheritance.

<<inference functions>>=
def lexer_for_chunk(name):
    """Return the Pygments lexer for chunk ``name``, or None.

    A user-supplied rule matching the whole name wins first.
    Otherwise the chunk name may use noweb's ``[[...]]`` quoting and
    may contain directory separators; only the basename decides, and
    names the built-in tables do not cover are matched against
    Pygments' filename patterns when Pygments is installed.
    """
    for pattern, lexer in LEXER_RULES:
        if pattern.search(name):
            return lexer
    if name.startswith("[[") and name.endswith("]]"):
        name = name[2:-2]
    basename = name.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
    if basename in LEXERS_BY_NAME:
        return LEXERS_BY_NAME[basename]
    dot = basename.rfind(".")
    if dot >= 0 and basename[dot:] in LEXERS_BY_EXT:
        return LEXERS_BY_EXT[basename[dot:]]
    return pygments_lexer_for_chunk(basename)
@

\subsection{Falling back on Pygments}
\label{sec:pygments}

Any language the tables miss can still be recognized, provided
Pygments is importable where the filter runs.  Pygments maintains
exactly the mapping we need---filename patterns to lexers---for
every language it supports, and since Pygments is what a
highlighter downstream eventually runs, an alias found this way is
one such a consumer accepts by construction.  The import is guarded: when Pygments is absent, the
fallback silently reports no match and chunks of unknown language
simply stay unannotated, which is the same graceful degradation the
rest of the inference already practices.

<<inference constants>>=
try:
    from pygments.lexers import find_lexer_class_for_filename
except ImportError:
    find_lexer_class_for_filename = None
@

Of a found lexer we keep the \emph{first} alias.  Any alias would
select the same lexer, but the annotation should name each language
one way only, so that a consumer keying on the value---or deriving a
name from it---sees one spelling per language.

Each match scans Pygments' full pattern list and may import lexer
modules, so the result is memoized per basename.  The cache also
stores misses: the names that recur most---ordinary chunk names
like [[functions]], looked up once per use edge during
propagation---are exactly the ones that match nothing.

<<inference constants>>=
PYGMENTS_ALIASES = {}
@

<<inference functions>>=
def pygments_lexer_for_chunk(basename):
    """Return the alias of Pygments' lexer for ``basename``, or None."""
    if find_lexer_class_for_filename is None:
        return None
    if basename not in PYGMENTS_ALIASES:
        cls = find_lexer_class_for_filename(basename)
        PYGMENTS_ALIASES[basename] = (
            cls.aliases[0] if cls is not None and cls.aliases else None)
    return PYGMENTS_ALIASES[basename]
@

\subsection{Reading the markup stream}

Every line of the stream is [[@keyword]] optionally followed by a
space and an argument.  We keep the raw lines for verbatim passthrough
and parse them on demand; parsing returns [[None]] for a missing
argument so that callers can distinguish [[@text]] (no argument at
all) from [[@text ]] (empty argument).

<<inference functions>>=
def parse(line):
    """Split a markup line into (keyword, argument).

    The argument is None when the line is only ``@keyword``.
    """
    space = line.find(" ")
    if space < 0:
        return line[1:], None
    return line[1:space], line[space + 1:]
@

\subsection{Pass 1: chunk languages}
\label{sec:langs}

We scan the stream once to learn, for every code chunk, its name
([[@defn]]) and which chunks it uses ([[@use]]).  Chunks named like
filenames seed the language map; the languages then propagate along
the use edges, because a chunk included into a Python file contains
Python.  A chunk used from chunks of two different languages is
ambiguous, so we mark it conflicting and leave it unannotated
rather than guess wrong.  Two further sources of seeds---a chunk's own
shebang line, and the lexical context a chunk is \emph{used} in---are
the subject of Section~\ref{sec:context}; the scan below already
calls into them.

<<inference constants>>=
CONFLICT = object()
@

<<inference functions>>=
def chunk_languages(lines):
    """Compute chunk languages and string-embedded use sites.

    Returns a pair ``(langs, plain_uses)``.  ``langs`` maps chunk
    names to Pygments lexer names: languages are seeded from
    ``@language`` annotations, filename-like chunk names, shebang
    lines, and the lexical context each chunk is used in (heredoc
    bodies, interpreter ``-c`` arguments, make recipes), then
    propagated to the chunks they use, transitively; chunks with
    conflicting inherited languages are omitted.  ``plain_uses``
    is the set of indices into ``lines`` of the ``@use`` lines
    that sit inside a Python string literal and must be rendered
    as plain text.
    """
    uses = {}
    langs = {}
    explicit = set()
    edges = []
    <<collect definitions, uses and string contexts>>
    <<propagate languages along use edges>>
    <<identify string-embedded use sites>>
    return ({name: lang for name, lang in langs.items()
             if lang is not CONFLICT},
            plain_uses)
@

A [[@defn]] names the chunk that the following lines (until
[[@end code]]) belong to, so we track the current chunk while inside
a code chunk and attribute every [[@use]] to it.

A chunk may also carry an explicit [[@language]] annotation, put
there by an earlier filter---typically an earlier run of [[autolang]]
itself (Section~\ref{sec:annotate}).  An annotation is better information
than anything we could infer, so it overrides name-based seeding
(it is collected after the [[@defn]] of its chunk) and is exempted
from the conflict rule during propagation: the [[explicit]] set
records which chunks are spoken for.  Annotations follow the
pipeline grammar of the noweb Hacker's Guide, whose suggested
values are language \emph{names}; the synonym table maps the names
that differ from the Pygments alias we need.

<<inference constants>>=
LANGUAGE_SYNONYMS = {
    "c++": "cpp",
    "sh": "bash",
    "caml": "ocaml",
}
@

Alongside the use edges we track whether each use site sits inside
a string literal: per chunk name, we toggle a flag on every line
whose text contains an odd number of triple-quote delimiters.  The
state is kept per \emph{name} rather than per chunk instance
because noweb concatenates same-named chunks when tangling, so a
string opened in one instance is still open in the next.  Each use
is recorded twice: as an edge for the propagation, and---together
with its line index and string context---in [[edges]], from which
the plain-text use sites are computed once the languages are known.

<<collect definitions, uses and string contexts>>=
current = None
in_code = False
in_string = {}
<<initialize lexical-context tracking>>
for index, line in enumerate(lines):
    keyword, arg = parse(line)
    if keyword == "begin" and arg.startswith("code"):
        in_code = True
        current = None
    elif keyword == "end" and arg.startswith("code"):
        in_code = False
        current = None
    elif in_code and keyword == "defn":
        current = arg
        uses.setdefault(current, set())
        in_string.setdefault(current, False)
        previous_line = line_so_far = ""
        if current not in explicit:
            lexer = lexer_for_chunk(current)
            if lexer is not None:
                langs[current] = lexer
    elif in_code and keyword == "language" and current is not None:
        langs[current] = LANGUAGE_SYNONYMS.get(arg, arg)
        explicit.add(current)
    elif in_code and keyword == "text" and current is not None:
        flips = sum((arg or "").count(d) for d in STRING_DELIMITERS)
        if flips % 2:
            in_string[current] = not in_string[current]
        <<seed a chunk's language from its shebang>>
        line_so_far += arg or ""
    elif in_code and keyword == "use" and current is not None:
        uses[current].add((arg, in_string[current]))
        edges.append((current, arg, in_string[current], index))
        <<label a chunk used in a foreign-language context>>
    elif in_code and keyword == "nl" and current is not None:
        previous_line, line_so_far = line_so_far, ""
@

The propagation is a straightforward worklist algorithm.  A chunk
whose own name determines a lexer keeps it, as does a chunk with an
[[@language]] annotation: explicit information is better than
inheritance, and skipping such chunks also stops the propagation
from crossing language boundaries between two root files.

<<propagate languages along use edges>>=
worklist = list(langs)
while worklist:
    parent = worklist.pop()
    lang = langs[parent]
    if lang is CONFLICT:
        continue
    for child, embedded in uses.get(parent, ()):
        if child in explicit or lexer_for_chunk(child) is not None:
            continue
        if embedded and lang in PYTHON_LEXERS:
            inherited = "text"
        else:
            inherited = lang
        if child not in langs:
            langs[child] = inherited
            worklist.append(child)
        elif langs[child] is not CONFLICT and langs[child] != inherited:
            langs[child] = CONFLICT
@

\subsection{Chunk uses inside Python strings}
\label{sec:strings}

A literate Python program may put a docstring's text in a chunk of
its own:

\begin{verbatim}
def fib(n):
    """
    <fib docstring>
    """
    ...
\end{verbatim}
(angle brackets doubled in the real source).  Treating that use like
any other, the docstring chunk would inherit the Python lexer, yet it
holds prose, not code: a highlighter would colour words like [[for]]
and [[is]] as keywords, and an autodefs filter would index whatever in
the prose happens to look like a definition.  The use site sits inside
an open string literal, and that is the diagnosis: a chunk used inside
a string in a Python chunk inherits the plain [[text]] lexer instead
of Python, which every consumer treats as unhighlighted, unindexed
text.

The parity heuristic behind the string tracking is deliberately naive:
a triple quote inside a comment or a single-quoted string fools it, and
it does not distinguish [[''']] from [["""]] when pairing delimiters.
Every failure mode merely degrades to the behaviour without the
heuristic, and the common literate pattern---docstring delimiters on
their own lines around the use---is handled correctly.  It is also
Python-only: a chunk used inside a string literal of another language
goes undetected and inherits that language.  Tracking every language's
string syntax is not worth the complexity for what is, in other
languages, a rare way to factor a program.  The conflict rule composes
naturally: a chunk used both inside a docstring and as real code
inherits [[text]] and a code lexer, conflicts, and stays unannotated.

The scan also records, per use, whether it sat inside a string.  The
annotator has no use for that second result---it labels chunks, not
use sites---but a renderer sharing this inference must know which
references sit inside strings, where it cannot typeset them as links,
so [[chunk_languages]] returns both.

<<inference constants>>=
PYTHON_LEXERS = {"python", "python3", "py", "py3"}
STRING_DELIMITERS = ('"""', "'''")
@

<<identify string-embedded use sites>>=
plain_uses = set()
for parent, child, embedded, index in edges:
    if embedded and langs.get(parent) in PYTHON_LEXERS:
        plain_uses.add(index)
@

\subsection{Language from lexical context}
\label{sec:context}

Name-based seeding answers \enquote{what language is this chunk?}
from the chunk's \emph{name}.  But a chunk's language is sometimes
fixed by \emph{where it is used}, not what it is called.  A chunk
spliced into a shell script as the body of a here-document, or handed
to [[python3 -c]], is written in the here-document's or the
interpreter's language regardless of its name; and a chunk whose name
is not filename-like---[[usage-formatter Python]], say---gets no seed
at all, yet may carry a [[#!]] line that states its language outright.

Leaving such chunks unlabeled is not merely a missed highlight.  An
unlabeled chunk is scanned by \emph{every} autodefs filter, because
the shared skip rule withholds only chunks annotated as some
\emph{other} language.  A Python helper left unlabeled is therefore
parsed as a Makefile by [[autodefs.make]], whose rule grammar matches
any column-zero \enquote{word\dots:} line, so Python's [[for]], [[if]]
and [[try]] lines surface as bogus index entries.  Reading the
language from context closes that gap at the source.

The four contexts share one table mapping an interpreter command---under
any path---to a lexer name.

<<inference constants>>=
import re

INTERPRETERS = {
    "python": "python", "python3": "python", "python2": "python",
    "sh": "bash", "bash": "bash", "dash": "bash", "ksh": "bash",
    "zsh": "bash", "perl": "perl", "ruby": "ruby",
    "node": "javascript", "nodejs": "javascript",
    "awk": "awk", "gawk": "awk", "sed": "sed", "lua": "lua",
    "php": "php", "Rscript": "r", "psql": "sql", "mysql": "sql",
    "sqlite3": "sql",
}
@

<<inference functions>>=
def interpreter_language(word):
    """Return the lexer name for interpreter ``word``, or None.

    Any directory prefix is ignored, so ``/usr/bin/python3`` and
    ``python3`` map alike.
    """
    return INTERPRETERS.get(word.rsplit("/", 1)[-1])
@

\paragraph{Shebang lines.}  A chunk whose name yields no language but
whose first line is a [[#!]] interpreter line is as self-describing as
a filename.  We read the interpreter---following the [[env]]
indirection when present---and seed the chunk from it.  This is what
gives a login-shell script chunk such as [[group-session]] or
[[team-report]] (named for the file it tangles to, but with no
recognised extension) its [[bash]] lexer.

<<inference functions>>=
def shebang_language(text):
    """Return the language a ``#!`` line names, or None."""
    if not text.startswith("#!"):
        return None
    tokens = text[2:].split()
    if not tokens:
        return None
    interpreter = tokens[0]
    if interpreter.rsplit("/", 1)[-1] == "env" and len(tokens) > 1:
        interpreter = tokens[1]
    return interpreter_language(interpreter)
@

The seed is applied the first time we see text for a chunk, and only
when neither an [[@language]] annotation nor the chunk's name has
already spoken for it---both are better evidence than a shebang.  We
record the chunk so its continuation parts (noweb concatenates
same-named chunks) do not re-test it, and add it to [[explicit]] so
the propagation treats the shebang as the chunk's settled language.

<<seed a chunk's language from its shebang>>=
if current not in shebang_checked:
    shebang_checked.add(current)
    if current not in explicit and lexer_for_chunk(current) is None:
        shebang = shebang_language(arg or "")
        if shebang is not None:
            langs[current] = shebang
            explicit.add(current)
@

\paragraph{Here-document bodies.}  A here-document factored into its
own chunk is recognised from the opener on the preceding line.  The
opener regular expression writes the two left angle brackets as
[[<{2}]] on purpose: a literal pair would be read by noweb as the
start of a chunk reference.  The command is the opener's first word
that is not a [[VAR=value]] environment-assignment prefix.

<<inference constants>>=
HEREDOC_RE = re.compile(r'<{2}[-~]?\s*(["\'\\]?)([A-Za-z_]\w*)\1\s*$')
ASSIGNMENT_RE = re.compile(r'\w+=')
@

<<inference functions>>=
def heredoc_opener(line):
    """Return ``(command, delimiter)`` if ``line`` opens a heredoc."""
    match = HEREDOC_RE.search(line)
    if match is None:
        return None
    command = ""
    for token in line.split():
        if ASSIGNMENT_RE.match(token):
            continue
        command = token
        break
    return command, match.group(2)
@

The body's language comes first from the interpreter that introduces
the heredoc (a [[python3 -]] feeding a here-document) and, failing
that, from the delimiter itself: a delimiter such as [[PYEOF]] or
[[SQL_END]] names its language in a conventional prefix, which we read
after stripping a trailing [[EOF]]/[[END]]-style suffix.  When neither
speaks, the body is labeled [[text]]---unhighlighted, and skipped by
every autodefs filter, the safe answer for opaque data.

<<inference constants>>=
DELIMITER_TAGS = {
    "py": "python", "python": "python", "python3": "python",
    "sh": "bash", "bash": "bash", "sql": "sql",
    "js": "javascript", "node": "javascript",
    "rb": "ruby", "ruby": "ruby", "pl": "perl", "perl": "perl",
    "lua": "lua", "php": "php", "awk": "awk", "sed": "sed", "r": "r",
}
@

<<inference functions>>=
def delimiter_language(delimiter):
    """Return the language a heredoc delimiter names, or None."""
    tag = delimiter.lower()
    for suffix in ("eof", "end", "eot", "doc", "here", "text"):
        if tag.endswith(suffix) and tag != suffix:
            tag = tag[:-len(suffix)]
            break
    return DELIMITER_TAGS.get(tag)
@

<<inference functions>>=
def heredoc_language(command, delimiter):
    """Return a heredoc body's language: interpreter, delimiter, text."""
    return (interpreter_language(command)
            or delimiter_language(delimiter)
            or "text")
@

\paragraph{Interpreter \texttt{-c} and \texttt{-e} arguments.}  The
same idea covers a one-liner whose program is a chunk: [[python -c]]
or [[perl -e]] followed by the chunk in quotes.  Here the use sits on
the interpreter's own line, right after the opening quote, so we match
the line up to the use.  The chunk must be the first thing inside the
quoted argument; a program with literal text before the chunk is not
recognised.

<<inference constants>>=
INLINE_CODE_RE = re.compile(r'(\S+)\s+-[ce]\s+["\']?\s*$')
@

<<inference functions>>=
def interpreter_argument_language(before):
    """Language of an interpreter run ``cmd -c``/``-e`` here, or None.

    ``before`` is the text preceding the chunk use on its own line.
    """
    match = INLINE_CODE_RE.search(before)
    if match is None:
        return None
    return interpreter_language(match.group(1))
@

\paragraph{Make recipes.}  Inside a [[make]] chunk, the recipe lines
of a target are shell, not make.  Inline recipe text is already safe
([[autodefs.make]] skips tab-indented lines), but a recipe factored
into its own chunk would otherwise inherit [[make]] and be scanned by
the wrong filter.  An indented use inside a make chunk is such a
recipe body, and we label it [[bash]]: in a Makefile only recipes are
indented, and the markup has already turned the recipe's leading tab
into spaces, so any leading whitespace marks the use as a recipe.

\paragraph{One decision.}  All four contexts answer the same
question---what language does this use site impose on the chunk---so a
single function decides it, given the text on the use's own line, the
preceding logical line, and the using chunk's language.

<<inference functions>>=
def context_language(before, previous, using_language):
    """Language a use site imposes on the used chunk, or None.

    ``before`` is the text before the use on its line, ``previous``
    the preceding logical line, ``using_language`` the language of
    the chunk doing the using.
    """
    if before.strip():
        return interpreter_argument_language(before)
    opener = heredoc_opener(previous)
    if opener is not None:
        return heredoc_language(*opener)
    if before and using_language == "make":
        return "bash"
    return None
@

