\subsection{Extending the tables from the command line}
\label{sec:mappings}


Additional lexer mappings can be passed as arguments of the form
[[.ext=lexer]] or [[name=lexer]], e.g.\
[[-filter 'autolang .rs=rust']].  Keys starting with a dot extend
the extension table, all others the basename table.

A third form, [[re:regex=lexer]], adds a rule rather than a table
entry: the regex is matched against the whole chunk name, so it can
label chunks whose names are not filenames---for example giving
[[python]] to every chunk that embeds a [[.py]] file behind a
label.  We split on the \emph{last} [[=]] so the regex may itself
contain one, and match with [[re.search]] so an unanchored pattern
need only occur somewhere in the name.  The parsing is part of the
shared inference so that every consumer of the tables accepts the same
argument forms.

<<inference functions>>=
def add_mapping(argument):
    """Add a lexer rule or table mapping from a command-line argument.

    ``re:regex=lexer`` appends a rule matched against the whole
    chunk name; ``.ext=lexer`` and ``name=lexer`` extend the
    extension and basename tables.  Returns False when ``argument``
    is none of those forms.
    """
    if argument.startswith("re:"):
        pattern, sep, value = argument[3:].rpartition("=")
        if not sep:
            return False
        LEXER_RULES.append((re.compile(pattern), value))
        return True
    key, sep, value = argument.partition("=")
    if not sep:
        return False
    if key.startswith("."):
        LEXERS_BY_EXT[key] = value
    else:
        LEXERS_BY_NAME[key] = value
    return True
@

\subsection{Annotating the stream}
\label{sec:annotate}


The main program runs pass~1 and then emits one of two views of its
result, chosen by a [[-list]] flag.  The string-embedded use sites
feed neither view---they matter only for rendering---so the second
half of [[chunk_languages]]' result is dropped in both.

Without [[-list]] the program annotates: it copies the stream back
out with an [[@language]] line on every classified chunk, the job
described above.  With [[-list]] it instead prints just the distinct
languages it found, one per line, and copies nothing.  That second
view exists for [[noweave]]'s sake.  A weave must decide \emph{which}
[[autodefs]] filters to splice into its pipeline before any markup
flows through it, yet only this inference knows which languages a
document actually contains.  So [[noweave -autolang -index]] runs
[[autolang -list]] as a pre-pass solely to learn that set, then adds
one [[autodefs]] filter per language (see \texttt{noweave.nw}).  Sorting
makes the output order independent of how the chunks happened to
hash, which keeps the assembled pipeline---and the weave's [[-v]]
trace of it---reproducible.

<<autolang functions>>=
def autolang_main():
    """Annotate a markup stream, or list its languages with ``-list``."""
    list_languages = False
    for argument in sys.argv[1:]:
        if argument in ("-list", "--list-languages"):
            list_languages = True
        elif not add_mapping(argument):
            print("autolang: unknown argument:", argument,
                  file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(1)
    lines = sys.stdin.read().split("\n")
    if lines and lines[-1] == "":
        lines.pop()
    langs, _ = chunk_languages(lines)
    if list_languages:
        for language in sorted(set(langs.values())):
            print(language)
    else:
        for line in annotate(lines, langs):
            print(line)
@

The annotation is inserted directly after a chunk's [[@defn]] line,
so it precedes every [[@text]] line of the chunk and a streaming
consumer knows the language before it sees any code.  The grammar
allows at most one [[@language]] per [[@begin code]]/[[@end code]]
pair, so a chunk that is already annotated---for instance because
[[autolang]] ran twice---is left alone; [[chunk_languages]] has
already treated the existing annotation as authoritative, making a
second run a no-op rather than an error.

<<autolang functions>>=
def annotate(lines, langs):
    """Return ``lines`` with @language added to known chunks."""
    out = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(lines):
        keyword, arg = parse(lines[i])
        if keyword == "begin" and arg.startswith("code"):
            <<copy the chunk, annotating its language>>
        else:
            out.append(lines[i])
            i += 1
    return out
